            "text": "{\n  \"relation\": \"REFINED\",\n  \"consensus_summary\": \"The helmet primarily symbolizes the believer's assurance or eschatological hope of salvation protecting the mind, while recognized alternatives view it as God's own divine warrior armor or Christ himself.\",\n  \"book_summary\": \"The helmet is Yeshua (Christ) himself, representing God's own war-dress that is lent to the believer, functioning as the covering for the head, which is Christ.\",\n  \"consensus_core_identification\": \"The believer's assurance/hope of salvation, or the objective saving power of God.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"Christ (Yeshua) as God's own war-dress lent to the believer.\",\n  \"core_relation\": \"BOOK_NARROWS\",\n  \"extension_relation\": \"BOOK_SUBSET\",\n  \"boundary_disagreement\": \"The book excludes the subjective, psychological interpretation (assurance protecting thoughts) prevalent in popular consensus, focusing exclusively on the objective christological and divine-warrior interpretations recognized as established alternatives.\",\n  \"material_contradiction\": \"None; the book relies on the divine warrior and christological readings that are explicitly attested in the consensus.\",\n  \"rationale\": \"The book builds upon specific alternative strands in the consensus (the Isaiah 59 divine warrior motif and the christological identification), emphasizing the objective reality of Christ as the armor rather than the primary consensus view of subjective psychological assurance.\",\n  \"citation_support\": \"STRONG\",\n  \"strongest_objection\": \"The book's reliance on a Hebrew wordplay (yeshuah/Yeshua) and its specific linkage to 1 Corinthians 11:3 ('the head of every man is Christ') to identify the helmet directly with Jesus goes beyond standard exegetical methods for interpreting Paul's Greek text in Ephesians 6.\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
